{
  "beruf": "gastronomie",
  "title": "Restaurants and kitchens",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Restaurants, brasseries and fast-food chains employ many newcomers in France, from the plonge to the salle. This deck covers the kitchen and its equipment, daily tasks, orders and service, food hygiene, and what you say to guests and to your team. In the kitchen colleagues usually say tu and shout short words; with guests you say vous.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a restaurant or a kitchen in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Commis de cuisine",
    "Plongeur / plongeuse",
    "Serveur / serveuse",
    "Chef de rang",
    "Cuisinier / cuisinière",
    "Employé polyvalent de restauration rapide / employée polyvalente de restauration rapide"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "A French kitchen speaks in short shouts. Learn them first: « Chaud devant ! », « Ça marche ! », « Oui chef ! ». When you don't understand, say « Pardon, chef, je n'ai pas compris. »",
    "Colleagues in the kitchen mostly say tu and use first names. With guests always say vous: « Bonsoir, vous avez choisi ? », « Je vous apporte l'addition. »",
    "If a guest asks about allergens and you are not sure, never guess: « Un instant, je demande en cuisine. » Ask your manager where the allergen list is kept.",
    "Food-hygiene training (often called HACCP) is usually asked for in restaurants. Ask your employer, France Travail or a union what you need. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
